 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 3.01 — Curves, Arc Length and Curvature\n\nStage 2 ended at the limit of the Erlangen programme. Its three geometries were **homogeneous** — every point like every other — and that is a severe restriction. Most shapes are lumpy, have no symmetry group worth the name, and cannot be described by \"a space and a group acting on it\".\n\nStage 3 starts again from the other end. Instead of a global group we work **locally**, attaching numbers to each point of a shape and asking how they vary. Curvature stops being a constant chosen from $\\{-1, 0, +1\\}$ and becomes a *function*.\n\nWe begin with the simplest curved objects. A **parametrised curve** is a smooth map\n\n$$\\gamma : I \\to \\mathbb R^n, \\qquad I \\subseteq \\mathbb R \\text{ an interval},$$\n\nand it is **regular** if $\\gamma'(t) \\ne 0$ everywhere — no instantaneous stops, which would allow corners.\n\nTwo ideas carry the notebook:\n\n**Arc length is the intrinsic parameter.** A curve can be parametrised in infinitely many ways, and none of them is the curve. What is intrinsic is *distance travelled along it*, and reparametrising by arc length removes the arbitrariness — at the cost of integrals that are usually impossible in closed form.\n\n**Curvature is the rate of turning.** For a unit-speed curve, $|\\gamma'| = 1$, so $\\gamma'$ only rotates. The size of its rate of change,\n\n$$\\kappa = |\\gamma''|,$$\n\nmeasures how fast the direction is turning — zero for a straight line, constant for a circle. This is the first genuinely *local* invariant in this journey: a number attached to each point rather than to the whole figure.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Arc length, and reparametrising by it\n\nThe length of $\\gamma$ from $t_0$ to $t$ is\n\n$$s(t) = \\int_{t_0}^{t} |\\gamma'(u)|\\,du,$$\n\nand regularity gives $s'(t) = |\\gamma'(t)| > 0$, so $s$ is strictly increasing and invertible. Substituting $t = t(s)$ produces the **arc-length** (or **unit-speed**) parametrisation, characterised by\n\n$$|\\gamma'(s)| = 1 .$$\n\n**Why insist on it.** Because it makes the formulas intrinsic. A curve traversed twice as fast is the same curve; any quantity defined from $\\gamma'$ and $\\gamma''$ directly would change under that reparametrisation, but anything defined in the unit-speed parametrisation cannot, since arc length is determined by the curve itself.\n\n**Why it is a nuisance.** The integral $\\int|\\gamma'|$ is rarely elementary. The circle and the helix work out; the ellipse does not — its arc length is an *elliptic integral*, which is where the name comes from. So the practical approach is:\n\n> Derive results in the unit-speed parametrisation, then convert them to formulas valid for **any** regular parametrisation.\n\nDoing this for curvature gives\n\n$$\\kappa(t) = \\frac{|\\gamma' \\times \\gamma''|}{|\\gamma'|^3},$$\n\nwhich needs no reparametrisation at all. We derive it in §2 and check it against the unit-speed definition.\n\nNote the cross product doing geometric work again, and note the $|\\gamma'|^3$: under $t \\mapsto \\lambda t$, the numerator scales by $\\lambda^3$ and the denominator by $\\lambda^3$, so $\\kappa$ is unchanged. That cancellation *is* the reparametrisation-invariance, made visible.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(u,v) := [u[2]*v[3]-u[3]*v[2], u[3]*v[1]-u[1]*v[3], u[1]*v[2]-u[2]*v[1]]$\nvnorm(u)    := sqrt( ratsimp(u . u) )$\nD(g, var)   := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), g)$        /* differentiate a curve */\n\n/* Speed and arc length */\nspeed(g, var)  := trigsimp( vnorm(D(g, var)) )$\narclen(g, var, t0, t1) := integrate( speed(g, var), var, t0, t1 )$\n\n/* --- The circle: speed r, length 2πr --- */\nassume(r > 0)$\ncirc : [r*cos(t), r*sin(t), 0]$\nprint(\"circle of radius r:\")$\nprint(\"   speed      = \", speed(circ, t))$\nprint(\"   arc length = \", arclen(circ, t, 0, 2*%pi))$\n\n/* --- The helix: constant speed, so arc length is easy --- */\nassume(aa > 0, bb > 0)$\nhelix : [aa*cos(t), aa*sin(t), bb*t]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"helix (a cos t, a sin t, b t):\")$\nprint(\"   speed      = \", speed(helix, t), \"   — constant, so unit-speed after scaling\")$\nprint(\"   arc length over one turn = \", arclen(helix, t, 0, 2*%pi))$\n\n/* Reparametrise the helix by arc length: s = √(a²+b²) t */\nc0 : sqrt(aa^2 + bb^2)$\nhelix_s : subst(t = s/c0, helix)$\nprint(\"   unit-speed form: \", helix_s)$\nprint(\"   its speed = \", trigsimp(speed(helix_s, s)), \"  ✓\")$\n\n/* --- The ellipse: arc length is NOT elementary --- */\nellipse : [3*cos(t), 2*sin(t), 0]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"ellipse (3cos t, 2sin t):\")$\nprint(\"   speed = \", speed(ellipse, t))$\nprint(\"   arc length over a quarter, numerically = \",\n      float( quad_qags( speed(ellipse,t), t, 0, %pi/2 )[1] ))$\nprint(\"   — the integral is elliptic, and has no elementary closed form\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "circle of radius r:\n   speed      =  r\n   arc length =  2*%pi*r\nhelix (a cos t, a sin t, b t):\n   speed      =  sqrt(bb^2+aa^2)    — constant, so unit-speed after scaling\n   arc length over one turn =  2*%pi*sqrt(bb^2+aa^2)\n   unit-speed form:\n                    [aa*cos(s/sqrt(bb^2+aa^2)),aa*sin(s/sqrt(bb^2+aa^2)),\n                     (bb*s)/sqrt(bb^2+aa^2)]\n   its speed =  1   ✓\nellipse (3cos t, 2sin t):\n   speed =  sqrt(5*sin(t)^2+4)\n   arc length over a quarter, numerically =  3.9663598973226466\n   — the integral is elliptic, and has no elementary closed form"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Curvature\n\nFor a unit-speed curve, $\\gamma' \\cdot \\gamma' = 1$; differentiating gives $\\gamma' \\cdot \\gamma'' = 0$. So **the acceleration of a unit-speed curve is always perpendicular to its velocity** — there is no tangential component left, because the speed is not changing. All the acceleration goes into turning, and\n\n$$\\kappa(s) = |\\gamma''(s)| \\;\\ge\\; 0$$\n\nmeasures exactly how much.\n\n**The general formula.** For an arbitrary regular parametrisation write $v = |\\gamma'|$ for the speed. Then $\\gamma' = v\\,T$ with $T$ the unit tangent, and\n\n$$\\gamma'' = v'\\,T + v\\,T' = v'\\,T + v^2\\kappa\\,N,$$\n\nsplitting acceleration into a tangential part (speeding up) and a normal part (turning). Taking the cross product kills the $T$ term:\n\n$$\\gamma' \\times \\gamma'' = vT \\times (v'T + v^2\\kappa N) = v^3\\kappa\\,(T \\times N),$$\n\nand $|T \\times N| = 1$ since they are orthonormal. Hence\n\n$$\\kappa = \\frac{|\\gamma' \\times \\gamma''|}{|\\gamma'|^3}.$$\n\nThat decomposition of acceleration into \"speeding up\" and \"turning\" is worth remembering: it is why a passenger feels cornering separately from braking, and it is the one-dimensional ancestor of the covariant derivative in 4.02, where the same split — tangential versus normal — defines what it means to differentiate along a curved space.\n\n**Expected values.** Line: $\\kappa = 0$. Circle of radius $r$: $\\kappa = 1/r$, so tight circles curve more. Helix: $\\kappa = a/(a^2+b^2)$, constant, reducing to $1/a$ when $b = 0$.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Curvature, in the two forms.  radcan canonicalises the surds, without\n   which equal expressions print in visibly different shapes. */\nsimpk(e) := radcan( trigsimp(e) )$\nkappa_gen(g, var) := simpk( vnorm(cross3(D(g,var), D(D(g,var),var)))\n                            / speed(g,var)^3 )$\nkappa_unit(g, var):= simpk( vnorm(D(D(g,var),var)) )$   /* valid only if |γ'| = 1 */\n\nprint(\"   curve                κ (general formula)\")$\nprint(\"   line                 \", kappa_gen([1+2*t, 3-t, 4*t], t))$\nprint(\"   circle radius r      \", kappa_gen(circ, t), \"   = 1/r ✓\")$\nprint(\"   helix                \", kappa_gen(helix, t), \"   = a/(a²+b²) ✓\")$\n\n/* The two definitions agree on the unit-speed helix */\nkh1 : kappa_unit(helix_s, s)$\nkh2 : kappa_gen(helix_s, s)$\nkh3 : kappa_gen(helix, t)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"helix, three routes to the same number:\")$\nprint(\"   |γ''| in unit-speed form   = \", kh1)$\nprint(\"   |γ'×γ''|/|γ'|³, unit-speed = \", kh2)$\nprint(\"   |γ'×γ''|/|γ'|³, original   = \", kh3)$\nprint(\"   differences: \", radcan(kh1 - kh2), \" and \", radcan(kh1 - kh3),\n      \"  — genuinely equal\")$\n\n/* Reparametrisation invariance, checked directly: traverse five times as fast */\nhelix_fast : subst(t = 5*t, helix)$\nkf : kappa_gen(helix_fast, t)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"traversing the helix five times as fast:\")$\nprint(\"   speed = \", speed(helix_fast, t), \"   (five times bigger)\")$\nprint(\"   κ     = \", kf, \"   difference from before: \", radcan(kf - kh3), \" ✓\")$\n\n/* The ellipse: curvature now VARIES along the curve */\nprint(\"\")$\nkell : kappa_gen(ellipse, t)$\nprint(\"ellipse (3cos t, 2sin t):  κ(t) = \", kell)$\nprint(\"   at t = 0    (end of major axis): κ = \", subst(t=0, kell),\n      \"   radius of curvature \", 1/subst(t=0, kell))$\nprint(\"   at t = π/2  (end of minor axis): κ = \", subst(t=%pi/2, kell),\n      \"   radius of curvature \", 1/subst(t=%pi/2, kell))$\nprint(\"   — sharpest at the ends of the major axis, flattest at the minor\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "   curve                κ (general formula)\n   line                  0\n   circle radius r       1/r    = 1/r ✓\n   helix                 aa/(bb^2+aa^2)    = a/(a²+b²) ✓\nhelix, three routes to the same number:\n   |γ''| in unit-speed form   =  aa/(bb^2+aa^2)\n   |γ'×γ''|/|γ'|³, unit-speed =  aa/(bb^2+aa^2)\n   |γ'×γ''|/|γ'|³, original   =  aa/(bb^2+aa^2)\n   differences:  0  and  0   — genuinely equal\ntraversing the helix five times as fast:\n   speed =  sqrt(25*bb^2+25*aa^2)    (five times bigger)\n   κ     =  aa/(bb^2+aa^2)    difference from before:  0  ✓\nellipse (3cos t, 2sin t):  κ(t) =  6/(5*sin(t)^2+4)^(3/2)\n   at t = 0    (end of major axis): κ =  3/4    radius of curvature  4/3\n   at t = π/2  (end of minor axis): κ =  2/9    radius of curvature  9/2\n   — sharpest at the ends of the major axis, flattest at the minor"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. Plane curves: signed curvature and the osculating circle\n\nFor a curve in the *plane* there is extra information available. The normal direction is determined up to sign, so we can fix it by rotating the tangent a quarter turn anticlockwise and define the **signed curvature**\n\n$$\\kappa_s = \\frac{x'y'' - y'x''}{(x'^2+y'^2)^{3/2}},$$\n\npositive where the curve turns left, negative where it turns right. This distinction is invisible in $\\mathbb R^3$, where $\\kappa = |\\kappa_s| \\ge 0$ always — a space curve can be rotated to reverse the apparent turn, a plane curve cannot without leaving the plane. Chirality, once again (1.07).\n\n**The osculating circle** at a point is the circle that best matches the curve there: same position, same tangent, same curvature. Its radius is $\\rho = 1/\\kappa$, the **radius of curvature**, and its centre lies at\n\n$$c = \\gamma + \\frac{1}{\\kappa}N$$\n\na distance $\\rho$ along the normal. It is the circle a car would trace if the steering were frozen at that instant.\n\n**Total turning.** For a simple closed plane curve traversed once anticlockwise,\n\n$$\\int \\kappa_s \\, ds = 2\\pi .$$\n\nThis is the **Umlaufsatz** (Hopf's theorem on turning tangents), and it is the first global statement of the stage: however the curve wiggles, the tangent direction makes exactly one full rotation. The integral of a local quantity is forced to a topological constant.\n\nThat should look familiar. It is the one-dimensional prototype of Gauss–Bonnet (3.08), where $\\int K\\,dA$ over a surface is likewise pinned to a topological invariant. Local geometry, integrated, yields topology — the pattern this whole stage is building towards.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Signed curvature of a plane curve (x(t), y(t)) */\nkappa_signed(x_, y_, var) := block([x1,x2,y1,y2],\n  x1 : diff(x_,var), x2 : diff(x1,var),\n  y1 : diff(y_,var), y2 : diff(y1,var),\n  trigsimp( (x1*y2 - y1*x2)/(x1^2 + y1^2)^(3/2) ) )$\n\nks_ell : kappa_signed(3*cos(t), 2*sin(t), t)$\nprint(\"ellipse, signed curvature = \", ks_ell)$\nprint(\"   positive throughout — traversed anticlockwise, always turning left\")$\n\n/* Reversing the orientation flips the sign */\nprint(\"   traversed backwards:      \", kappa_signed(3*cos(-t), 2*sin(-t), t))$\n\n/* Total turning: ∫ κ_s ds = ∫ κ_s |γ'| dt should be 2π */\nsp_ell : speed(ellipse, t)$\nturning : integrate( ratsimp(ks_ell * sp_ell), t, 0, 2*%pi )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"∫ κ_s ds around the ellipse = \", turning, \" = \", float(turning))$\nprint(\"2π = \", float(2*%pi), \"   — the Umlaufsatz ✓\")$\n\n/* Same for a lopsided closed curve, to show it is not an artefact of symmetry */\nxw : 2*cos(t) + cos(2*t)/2$\nyw : 3*sin(t) - sin(2*t)/3$\nks_w : kappa_signed(xw, yw, t)$\nsp_w : trigsimp( sqrt(diff(xw,t)^2 + diff(yw,t)^2) )$\nturn_w : float( quad_qags( ratsimp(ks_w*sp_w), t, 0, 2*%pi )[1] )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"a lopsided closed curve: ∫ κ_s ds = \", turn_w, \"   still 2π ✓\")$\n\n/* Osculating circles on the ellipse */\nosc_centre(x_, y_, var, t0) := block([x1,y1,sp2,ks,nx,ny,rho],\n  x1 : subst(var=t0, diff(x_,var)),  y1 : subst(var=t0, diff(y_,var)),\n  sp2: sqrt(x1^2 + y1^2),\n  ks : subst(var=t0, kappa_signed(x_,y_,var)),\n  nx : -y1/sp2, ny : x1/sp2,               /* left normal */\n  rho: 1/ks,\n  [ float(subst(var=t0,x_) + rho*nx), float(subst(var=t0,y_) + rho*ny), float(rho) ] )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"osculating circles on the ellipse:\")$\nfor t0 in [0, %pi/2] do block([oc],\n  oc : osc_centre(3*cos(t), 2*sin(t), t, t0),\n  print(\"   at t = \", float(t0), \":  centre \", [oc[1], oc[2]], \"   radius \", oc[3]) )$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "ellipse, signed curvature =  6/(5*sin(t)^2+4)^(3/2)\n   positive throughout — traversed anticlockwise, always turning left\n   traversed backwards:       -(6/(5*sin(t)^2+4)^(3/2))\n∫ κ_s ds around the ellipse =  2*%pi  =  6.283185307179586\n2π =  6.283185307179586    — the Umlaufsatz ✓\na lopsided closed curve: ∫ κ_s ds =  6.2831853071796155    still 2π ✓\nosculating circles on the ellipse:\n   at t =  0.0 :  centre  [1.6666666666666667,0.0]    radius\n          1.3333333333333333\n   at t =  1.5707963267948966 :  centre  [0.0,-2.5]    radius  4.5"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* The ellipse with its osculating circles at the ends of both axes */\nts : makelist(2*%pi*k/300, k, 0, 300)$\ncurve_pts(fx, fy) := [discrete, map(lambda([u], float(subst(t=u, fx))), ts),\n                                map(lambda([u], float(subst(t=u, fy))), ts)]$\ncirc_pts(cx, cy, rr) := [discrete,\n   map(lambda([u], cx + rr*cos(float(u))), ts),\n   map(lambda([u], cy + rr*sin(float(u))), ts)]$\n\no1 : osc_centre(3*cos(t), 2*sin(t), t, 0)$\no2 : osc_centre(3*cos(t), 2*sin(t), t, %pi/2)$\nprint(\"osculating circle at (3,0): centre \", [o1[1],o1[2]], \" radius \", o1[3])$\nprint(\"osculating circle at (0,2): centre \", [o2[1],o2[2]], \" radius \", o2[3])$\n\nplot2d([ curve_pts(3*cos(t), 2*sin(t)),\n         circ_pts(o1[1], o1[2], o1[3]),\n         circ_pts(o2[1], o2[2], o2[3]),\n         [discrete, [3.0, 0.0], [0.0, 2.0]] ],\n       [x, -5, 5], [y, -7.5, 4],\n       [style, [lines,3],[lines,1],[lines,1],[points,3]],\n       [legend, \"ellipse\", \"osculating circle at (3,0), ρ = 4/3\",\n                \"osculating circle at (0,2), ρ = 9/2\", \"points of contact\"],\n       [same_xy, true],\n       [title, \"osculating circles: tight where the curve bends sharply\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
