  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n| Quantity | Formula | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| arc length | $s(t) = \\int |\\gamma'|\\,du$ | rarely elementary |\n| unit-speed condition | $|\\gamma'| = 1$ | makes formulas intrinsic |\n| curvature | $\\kappa = |\\gamma''|$ (unit speed) | rate of turning |\n| general formula | $\\kappa = \\dfrac{|\\gamma'\\times\\gamma''|}{|\\gamma'|^3}$ | any parametrisation |\n| signed curvature (plane) | $\\dfrac{x'y''-y'x''}{(x'^2+y'^2)^{3/2}}$ | carries a sign |\n| radius of curvature | $\\rho = 1/\\kappa$ | radius of the osculating circle |\n| total turning | $\\oint \\kappa_s\\,ds = 2\\pi$ | the Umlaufsatz |\n\nVerified computationally: $\\kappa = 1/r$ for a circle, $0$ for a line, $a/(a^2+b^2)$ for a helix — the same value by three different routes, and unchanged when the helix is traversed five times as fast. The ellipse's curvature varies between $3/4$ and $2/9$, and its total turning is $2\\pi$ both symbolically and, for a deliberately lopsided closed curve, numerically.\n\n## What has changed since Stage 2\n\n**Curvature is now a function.** In Stage 2, $K$ was a constant labelling which of three geometries we were in. Here $\\kappa(t)$ varies from point to point along a single object, and the ellipse — a perfectly ordinary curve — has no constant curvature at all. That is the whole reason Klein's framework had to be abandoned: an ellipse has no transformation group taking any point to any other.\n\n**Local data integrates to global facts.** The Umlaufsatz is the first instance: $\\kappa_s$ is defined pointwise and knows nothing about the curve as a whole, yet its integral is forced to $2\\pi$ by the mere fact that the curve closes up. Every major theorem remaining in this journey has this shape — Gauss–Bonnet (3.08), the index theorems signposted in 6.07, and Chern–Weil (5.06), which is the general machine for turning local curvature into topological invariants.\n\n**Acceleration splits into tangential and normal parts.** $\\gamma'' = v'T + v^2\\kappa N$. The normal part is the curvature. When we move to surfaces in 3.04, the *same* split — decomposing a derivative into components tangent to the surface and normal to it — defines the second fundamental form, and in 4.02 it becomes the covariant derivative on an abstract manifold. One idea, three levels of generality.\n\n## Next\n\n**3.02 — Frenet–Serret and Torsion** completes the local theory of space curves. Curvature measures turning within a plane; **torsion** measures how fast that plane itself twists. Together they satisfy a system of ODEs, and the fundamental theorem of curves says the pair $(\\kappa, \\tau)$ determines the curve completely up to rigid motion — the exact analogue of 1.07's \"distances determine a configuration\".\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. Chapter 1 is the standard treatment of curves, and the source most of this stage follows.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 1 reviews curves and surfaces as motivation before the abstract theory of Stage 4.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. The most accessible undergraduate account; Chapters 1–2 cover this notebook, and it is in the same series as Anderson's *Hyperbolic Geometry* cited in 2.06.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 5 develops curves and then surfaces by generalising from them, in the same order used here.\n\nThe Umlaufsatz is due to Heinz Hopf (1935), who gave the first correct proof of what had been assumed obvious.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
